# Everything below is optional and independent. The console runs with the
# PingOne sign-in and Authenticate Agent buttons hidden until their block
# of variables is fully populated.

# --- OIDC (PingOne) — "Sign in with PingOne" -------------------------------
# Create a PingOne application (OIDC Web App) with:
#   Redirect URI:            http://localhost:8000/api/auth/callback
#   Post-logout redirect URI: http://localhost:5173
#   Grant type: Authorization Code, PKCE required (S256)
OIDC_DISCOVERY_URL=
OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
OIDC_POST_LOGOUT_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:5173
# Space-delimited scopes requested at sign-in. "openid" is required (it's
# what makes this OIDC rather than bare OAuth); add scopes here if the
# resulting access_token also needs to work as the `subject_token` for a
# broader token exchange than what profile/email alone would grant.
OIDC_SCOPES=openid profile email

# --- Agent identity — "Authenticate Agent" (Client Credentials + RFC 8693) -
# Create a SECOND PingOne application (Worker/Service app) with:
#   Grant types: Client Credentials, Token Exchange
#   Token endpoint auth method: client_secret_basic
AGENT_CLIENT_ID=
AGENT_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Space-delimited scope(s) for the agent's own actor token (step 1, Client
# Credentials) — what the agent can do as itself. Most PingOne worker apps
# require this explicitly, or the grant fails / returns an unusable token.
AGENT_SCOPES=
# Space-delimited scope(s) for the resulting *delegated* token (step 2,
# Token Exchange) — what the agent can do on behalf of the signed-in user.
# Optional: falls back to AGENT_SCOPES if unset, but is often a different,
# broader (or narrower) scope in a real deployment.
AGENT_TOKEN_EXCHANGE_SCOPE=
# The `aud` claim /api/invoke requires on the delegated token before it will
# talk to the agent (this is what makes "Authenticate Agent" mandatory
# rather than decorative — inbound auth re-verifies signature/issuer/expiry
# /audience fresh on every call, like AWS AgentCore's inbound auth authorizer
# does, rather than trusting a session). Optional: falls back to
# AGENT_CLIENT_ID, which is what PingOne populates `aud` with by default for
# a token minted against a custom resource owned by the agent app.
#
# IMPORTANT: this requires the PingOne resource behind AGENT_TOKEN_EXCHANGE_SCOPE
# to be configured to issue JWT (not opaque/reference) access tokens — inbound
# auth can only cryptographically verify a JWT.
AGENT_EXPECTED_AUDIENCE=

# --- Session cookie encryption (required if either block above is used) ---
# Any long random string; it's hashed to a 32-byte key for JWE A256GCM.
SESSION_SECRET=

# --- LangGraph agent --------------------------------------------------------
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
AGENT_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5

# --- A2A: delegation to the Task Agent (see task-agent/) --------------------
# The Chat Agent forwards its own delegated token to this URL — see
# CLAUDE.md "Identity propagation across the A2A hop". task-agent/.env's
# OIDC_DISCOVERY_URL / AGENT_EXPECTED_AUDIENCE must match this service's.
TASK_AGENT_URL=http://localhost:9010

# --- Networking --------------------------------------------------------------
APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000
FRONTEND_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173
